\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{A finite real self-adjoint spectral decomposition}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{Let #{(E,h)} be a real inner-product space of dimension #{n\geq0},
and let #{T:E\to E} be linear and [self-adjoint](fdip-001P). There is an
orthonormal basis #{u_1,\ldots,u_n} with real eigenvalues #{q_i}. Put
#{P_ix=h(u_i,x)u_i}. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
I&=\sum_iP_i,&T&=\sum_iq_iP_i,\\
P_iP_j&=\delta_{ij}P_i,&\det T&=\prod_iq_i.
\end{aligned}}
Moreover #{h(x,Tx)>0} for every nonzero #{x} exactly when every #{q_i>0}.
In that case #{T^{-1}=\sum_iq_i^{-1}P_i} is self-adjoint and positive.}
\proof{
\p{For #{n>0}, maximize the continuous function #{x\mapsto h(x,Tx)}
on the compact unit sphere. Let #{u} be a maximizing unit vector and
#{q=h(u,Tu)}. If #{v\perp u}, the Rayleigh quotient
##{\frac{h(u+tv,T(u+tv))}{1+t^2h(v,v)}}
has derivative zero at #{t=0}. Self-adjointness gives derivative
#{2h(v,Tu)}, so #{Tu-qu} is orthogonal both to #{u^\perp} and to #{u}.
It is zero. Also #{h(u,Tv)=h(Tu,v)=qh(u,v)=0} on #{u^\perp}, so
this complement is stable and the restriction is self-adjoint.
Induction gives the basis; the base case #{n=0} is the empty basis.}
\p{Orthonormal expansion proves the projector identities. On the top
exterior power, #{\bigwedge^nT} multiplies
#{u_1\wedge\cdots\wedge u_n} by #{\prod_iq_i}. This scalar is independent
of the nonzero top generator and is the intrinsic determinant.
Necessity of positive eigenvalues follows by evaluating at #{u_i};
sufficiency follows from
##{h(x,Tx)=\sum_iq_i h(u_i,x)^2.}
Multiplying the projector sums proves the asserted inverse and its positivity.
For #{n=0}, the empty product is one and the unique endomorphism is the
identity, invertible and vacuously positive.}
}
\p{This gives the operator proof of the real symmetric decomposition in
\citet{Appendix A.5.2, pp. 646-647}{boyd2004convex}.
Mathlib's \leanref{LinearMap.IsSymmetric.eigenvectorBasis} supplies an
orthonormal eigenbasis for a finite-dimensional inner-product space;
here its scalar field is specialized to #{\mathbb R}. The declaration
alone asserts neither Gaussian normalization nor a graph spectrum.}
